#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130114} :activity/id #uuid "92f7b628-011e-58aa-ab66-81a57a321eef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049272 :topic/id #uuid "e8e059cb-70d3-5192-9f7c-ec4083339ab1" :topic/math-academy-id 1994} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129215 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129034 :question/id #uuid "fdf51620-58e9-5886-93e5-02a814b154bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8905"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050217}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430042}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"} :step/id #uuid "dcc3ef91-e52c-5a24-9e81-8b2613846183" :step/math-academy-id 33265 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130115}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130112 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129216 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129035 :question/id #uuid "ae72af2a-ec7e-569d-89a8-550c8eeabdbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8909"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557311}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "86ce5fe6-7a22-5926-9ab2-a82592ddf97f" :step/math-academy-id 33278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129217 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129036 :question/id #uuid "fcfc9339-efd3-525f-a6f3-695124724e1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9120"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050393}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557230}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "c6514f45-14b6-5849-8cf5-f67e8581faff" :step/math-academy-id 33991 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130112}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130413 :tutorial/content "Consider the arc of the curve $y=f(x)$ that lies between the vertical lines $x=x_1$ and $x=x_2,$ as shown below on the left. By rotating this curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a **surface of revolution,** as shown below on the right. How can we find its surface area?\n\n![](images/d9/d929311a1d86da82fd2acb517ed148715273d37a66928f0cb2949353e06457e1.png)\n\nFirst, we divide the interval $[a,b]$ into small subintervals, each of length $\\Delta{x}.$ Let's consider a part of the curve that corresponds to one of those subintervals and let the length of the corresponding curved arc be $\\Delta{s}.$ When this part of the curve is rotated about the $x$-axis, it forms a closed strip. \n\n![](images/55/55ff01b354818b7ec3b9240d470acfa14c648b967f142b7554c172d12e2ca57b.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, we can assume that both left and right radii of the strip are approximately equal to $y=f(x).$ Then, this strip can be viewed as the lateral surface of a right cylinder with height $h=\\Delta{s}$ and radius $r=y=f(x).$ \n\n![](images/5a/5a71ce7729d37ca3d1fb86067f0d3f2233243a24710d08132dfc63f372bb6521.png)\n\nWe can approximate the lateral surface area of such a cylinder as\n\n$$\nS_{\\text{strip}} \\approx S_{\\text{cylinder}} = 2\\pi r \\cdot h = 2\\pi y \\cdot \\Delta{s}.\n$$\n\nThe sum over all subintervals will approximate the total area $S$ that we want. The approximation becomes more and more accurate as $\\Delta{s} \\to 0$. Therefore, to get the area of the surface, we need to integrate:\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nRecall that for a curve $y = f(x),$ the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find the total surface area using the formula\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7ed0d58-7855-54cd-952c-8f23fe9c6678" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5532 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9fe394ca-d35a-573c-8c77-ab458259c696" :step/math-academy-id 34375 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130414 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're given a curve of the form $x=f(y)$ that lies between the horizontal lines $y=y_1$ and $y=y_2.$ How do we calculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\nEarlier, we showed that the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nFor a curve $x = f(y),$ it can be shown that the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the surface that is obtained when the $x=f(y)$ curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1edfe684-3e13-5317-b7d2-d7f6cf246298" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5534 :tutorial/title "Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "f3f10e55-00c8-543e-a184-f85768a08ce8" :step/math-academy-id 34377 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130113}}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution: Rotation About the X-Axis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129339}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557360} :activity/id #uuid "b330a3da-2337-5f73-8dc7-b49431a18a90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049272 :topic/id #uuid "e8e059cb-70d3-5192-9f7c-ec4083339ab1" :topic/math-academy-id 1994} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557360 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0ba94d7-362e-54af-a4dc-5650b2efcf4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{csc}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386102} {:answer/id #uuid "0100602d-f98b-513d-9cb0-94a20d3703e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386103} {:answer/id #uuid "ea707e80-3f6b-5ca4-a2d4-29097932496c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386104} {:answer/id #uuid "4278f499-f5e5-5ac4-b8e0-fc854a2288e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386105} {:answer/id #uuid "028fc07b-3f60-56d1-8edb-1fb1620551ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386106} :answer-field/id #uuid "66b18467-8af9-5ad0-97da-81cd55579f69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5c12e390-7cac-5d15-8cb3-07a2b192a6de" :question/math-academy-id "q-104533" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{tan}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)={sec}^{2}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{({sec}^{2}⁡y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "54873476-a9e7-5950-916e-34407ff4a19a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e914b0d-41ca-50e1-920b-daa562691bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556570} {:answer/id #uuid "772b2fe0-64e6-5c8e-a51d-7d63a2be61f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556701} {:answer/id #uuid "bacbe449-5ccd-5971-8f8e-92d50f996dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556886} {:answer/id #uuid "d807f0fc-fb4a-570e-8af5-96a9583d0e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556964} {:answer/id #uuid "f181cf37-c4e6-5144-a534-9ebcde59a058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556886} :answer-field/id #uuid "54638d73-c9f4-5d31-93a7-16d80e13c507" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cbe7ea72-e04a-55e6-a4f7-365b15606888" :question/math-academy-id "q-109257" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ that lies between the lines $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=-1$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi [1-(-1)] \\\\  & =8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9b70f412-8e8e-5534-843c-01eda5a01abc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557396}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5126c3d8-978f-582d-85b3-1b273093c176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556615} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba1a2b2-1160-53df-9d34-b83861b916c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556764} {:answer/id #uuid "b59b2a2c-fb0b-5db0-9b18-977f28ffedf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556874} {:answer/id #uuid "d151476d-b4bb-5405-af19-fe054e71c98a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556935} {:answer/id #uuid "d70bd935-3591-5e22-b322-ce29e2d33415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556935} :answer-field/id #uuid "64fdecf4-7be9-5050-b772-01b00e36fa6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "325cb3ef-db56-5f90-98ae-212e45702d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109145" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cbbd7e33-220f-5b34-8cd9-a28d0cba0910" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557408}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "422bd46c-effb-5d0b-904d-3ee35544d973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430009} {:answer/id #uuid "53183f8e-14d6-52c2-9f1a-70be683a7846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430011} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5eaa2f-7b67-59fb-ac74-96008538d1e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430012} {:answer/id #uuid "a548f782-4430-54c1-9ade-29c1fd806b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430021} {:answer/id #uuid "c743a931-9156-5119-af96-2e6b26ebadfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430012} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e6cbf3-7b66-5401-9a6d-5259b70ef31f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e501ffe3-f914-5273-b8d8-73d2e5bc3adb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109269" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2}$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})=\\frac{1-x}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}=\\frac{1-2x+{x}^{2}}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1-2x+{x}^{2}}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3/2})\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}(3-x)}{3}(\\frac{x+1}{2\\sqrt{x}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3-x)(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3+2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {[3x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d708bf92-e97e-5304-9f82-56eda557c45f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93561cb9-31e8-5f50-b3bf-ac1e4c078018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430018} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7a244a-9ef8-56e4-ad31-1e3ad490a401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430020} {:answer/id #uuid "b0993602-e32e-5385-8615-215b677568b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430023} {:answer/id #uuid "c21c3926-fbd8-543d-9a89-059162436863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430024} {:answer/id #uuid "c6975ddc-3a08-5688-b3d1-0de4aad419be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430025} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd353f17-4f1a-5904-b62b-8c4277a7e856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5eff5095-a0c0-5448-9e3d-e45ec11b1792" :question/math-academy-id "q-109186" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a70a12201e99ded8eed85b797c760862814e1f0a50129020fa6d3b35d4334394.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that lies in the first quadrant between its $y$-intercepts (see figure above). What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =\\pm n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fac8209e-847f-5663-a33a-3c44580d23ed" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557385}}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution: Rotation About the X-Axis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556418}]}
